1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:04,370
Today, our mission is, um, exploring this invisible digital paper trail.

2
00:00:04,730 --> 00:00:07,390
Yeah, it is entirely invisible to most of us.

3
00:00:07,550 --> 00:00:07,690
Right.

4
00:00:08,510 --> 00:00:10,510
So how do massive IT systems

5
00:00:11,150 --> 00:00:13,370
actually prove they communicated with each other?

6
00:00:14,030 --> 00:00:21,030
Like, imagine an auditor walks in and asks you to prove a registration service wrote a learner's record on a specific day.

7
00:00:21,350 --> 00:00:23,110
Which happens way more often than you'd think.

8
00:00:23,350 --> 00:00:23,770
Exactly.

9
00:00:24,770 --> 00:00:25,290
Can you prove it?

10
00:00:26,110 --> 00:00:32,610
Well, as we'll discover, the answer depends entirely on choices you made, uh, before that system ever went live.

11
00:00:33,230 --> 00:00:36,010
And that is the fundamental rule of digital exchanges.

12
00:00:36,070 --> 00:00:39,190
I mean, you just cannot retroactively create evidence.

13
00:00:39,450 --> 00:00:40,330
No time machines allowed.

14
00:00:40,450 --> 00:00:48,850
Right. No time machines. If the proper tracking settings aren't chosen before that very first call between systems, well, the evidence simply does not exist.

15
00:00:49,290 --> 00:00:51,430
Okay, so let's unpack this a bit.

16
00:00:52,310 --> 00:00:56,930
If we look at a central data exchange platform, like, say, the X-Road system,

17
00:00:57,770 --> 00:01:02,249
the very first piece of evidence we need is the message log on a security server.

18
00:01:02,630 --> 00:01:03,730
Yeah, the message log.

19
00:01:04,150 --> 00:01:06,990
So how does that log actually capture the exchange?

20
00:01:07,510 --> 00:01:09,870
Well, you basically have three distinct settings.

21
00:01:10,050 --> 00:01:13,990
There is full logging, which, uh, keeps the entire message and its details.

22
00:01:14,110 --> 00:01:14,910
Okay, so everything.

23
00:01:15,130 --> 00:01:20,650
Exactly, everything. Then there is details only, which strips out the actual message and just keeps the metadata.

24
00:01:21,190 --> 00:01:23,930
And finally, you know, you can just turn logging completely off.

25
00:01:24,370 --> 00:01:25,770
Which sounds incredibly risky.

26
00:01:26,050 --> 00:01:35,430
Very. Crucially, though, the information mediator specification, which is essentially the governing rulebook here, it explicitly requires a signed, timestamped message log.

27
00:01:36,070 --> 00:01:38,450
If you wanna use it as definitive proof, right?

28
00:01:38,690 --> 00:01:45,670
Yes, exactly. Wait, so if I choose details only just to, I don't know, save server storage space,

29
00:01:46,350 --> 00:01:51,590
I essentially have a security camera that logs someone walked into the building, but it completely blurs out their face.

30
00:01:52,210 --> 00:01:53,510
That is a great way to put it, yeah.

31
00:01:54,570 --> 00:01:57,270
So why doesn't that blurred footage hold up for an auditor?

32
00:01:57,990 --> 00:02:00,090
It comes down to cryptographic verification.

33
00:02:00,650 --> 00:02:04,730
So with full logging, the complete records can be cryptographically verified after the fact.

34
00:02:05,090 --> 00:02:05,530
Oh, I see.

35
00:02:06,050 --> 00:02:12,090
Yeah. If you only have the details, you basically just have an envelope, but you have no proof of the actual letter inside.

36
00:02:12,790 --> 00:02:14,110
So the auditor is just guessing.

37
00:02:14,690 --> 00:02:19,230
Right. An auditor can't confirm what data was actually exchanged, which makes it totally useless as evidence.

38
00:02:19,610 --> 00:02:20,470
That makes perfect sense.

39
00:02:21,190 --> 00:02:23,890
So if full message logs secure the actual content,

40
00:02:24,630 --> 00:02:27,150
how do we monitor the network's overall pulse?

41
00:02:27,570 --> 00:02:28,870
Ah, without breaking privacy.

42
00:02:28,950 --> 00:02:30,790
Yeah, without snooping on those secure messages.

43
00:02:30,850 --> 00:02:32,810
That requires a totally different tool, doesn't it?

44
00:02:33,010 --> 00:02:37,270
It does. And that brings us to the second type of record, which is operational monitoring.

45
00:02:37,410 --> 00:02:37,630
Okay.

46
00:02:38,330 --> 00:02:44,230
This captures who called, which service they called, when they did it, how large the message was, and whether it succeeded.

47
00:02:44,470 --> 00:02:45,250
But no content.

48
00:02:45,870 --> 00:02:48,570
Never the content. It is strictly about access control.

49
00:02:49,070 --> 00:02:52,510
So a regular member only reads the operational records about itself.

50
00:02:52,970 --> 00:02:55,070
Through its own monitoring service, I assume?

51
00:02:55,330 --> 00:02:55,530
Right.

52
00:02:56,170 --> 00:03:01,010
Meanwhile, the owner of the security server can read the records of all clients on that specific server.

53
00:03:01,450 --> 00:03:03,150
Okay, so it's exactly like a phone bill.

54
00:03:03,470 --> 00:03:07,670
You get the timestamps and the endpoints, but the actual conversation remains totally private.

55
00:03:07,810 --> 00:03:09,070
Yes, exactly like a phone bill.

56
00:03:09,670 --> 00:03:10,910
But here's my question.

57
00:03:11,550 --> 00:03:16,890
If a regular member can only see their own records, how do system operators get a big picture view?

58
00:03:17,150 --> 00:03:19,890
Like, if a massive error occurs across the network.

59
00:03:20,530 --> 00:03:22,290
That is where the third record comes in.

60
00:03:22,390 --> 00:03:23,570
We call it the traffic view.

61
00:03:23,990 --> 00:03:24,930
Traffic view. Got it.

62
00:03:25,450 --> 00:03:27,930
This lives on the security server's diagnostics page.

63
00:03:28,210 --> 00:03:31,030
It draws this filterable graph of the requests that pass through.

64
00:03:31,290 --> 00:03:32,290
Like a literal chart.

65
00:03:32,470 --> 00:03:35,790
Yeah. By period, party, role, success or failure rate.

66
00:03:36,210 --> 00:03:39,450
It allows operators to spot traffic jams or failing nodes instantly.

67
00:03:39,870 --> 00:03:41,370
And they still never see the private data.

68
00:03:41,450 --> 00:03:48,790
Exactly. But, and this is a massive catch, this view only exists if the operational monitoring add-on is installed.

69
00:03:48,950 --> 00:03:52,490
Oh, wow. Yeah. If you skip the add-on, you are flying totally blind.

70
00:03:52,930 --> 00:03:56,510
Okay, let's look at a concrete example. Let's take the fictional country of Progessa

71
00:03:57,150 --> 00:03:59,350
as a sandbox to see how this plays out in real life.

72
00:03:59,570 --> 00:04:01,590
Progessa is a great example for this.

73
00:04:01,870 --> 00:04:08,890
Right. So if Progessa runs a major data exchange, like a student registration run, how do these three tools actually work together?

74
00:04:09,370 --> 00:04:13,750
Well, in Progessa's architecture, no member sees another member's records by default.

75
00:04:13,910 --> 00:04:14,269
None at all?

76
00:04:14,690 --> 00:04:19,730
None. The owner of the learner register only sees its own message logs and calls made to its service.

77
00:04:19,930 --> 00:04:20,730
Okay, makes sense.

78
00:04:21,070 --> 00:04:24,130
And the registration service only sees logs of the calls it makes.

79
00:04:24,590 --> 00:04:27,950
While the central system operator, I'm guessing, only reads the traffic view.

80
00:04:28,730 --> 00:04:32,370
Yes, the traffic view and the operational records their specific role allows.

81
00:04:33,370 --> 00:04:40,090
So does this mean everyone has to bring their own individual puzzle piece to the auditor just to prove a single transaction happened?

82
00:04:40,650 --> 00:04:43,190
It seems, I don't know, deliberately fragmented.

83
00:04:43,630 --> 00:04:47,650
It is siloed by design. It is actually a vital security and privacy feature.

84
00:04:47,970 --> 00:04:53,710
Really? Yeah. You never want a single node in a massive government network to have a God's eye view of everyone else's data.

85
00:04:54,270 --> 00:04:56,090
You only prove your side of the handshake.

86
00:04:57,010 --> 00:04:58,070
I guess that protects everyone.

87
00:04:58,430 --> 00:05:01,770
Exactly. Each reader can only prove what their specific records show.

88
00:05:02,630 --> 00:05:04,270
So what does this all mean for you?

89
00:05:04,890 --> 00:05:07,010
To genuinely prove a system call took place,

90
00:05:07,610 --> 00:05:08,870
you need all three records.

91
00:05:09,430 --> 00:05:12,330
The message log, operational monitoring, and the traffic view.

92
00:05:12,570 --> 00:05:21,490
Right. But the absolute golden rule is that full message logging, monitoring access, and that monitoring add-on must be enabled before day one.

93
00:05:21,930 --> 00:05:25,830
Because if you choose them late, those first months of calls leave absolutely no evidence.

94
00:05:25,910 --> 00:05:26,610
They are just gone.

